Create two coordinated end-of-match popup mockups for Urkaaaz: one VICTORY
state and one DEFEAT state.

## Reference inputs

- Attach a full-screen screenshot of the Urkaaaz match screen and battlefield
  for campaign level 1, `green_frontier`. Use it as the primary scene
  reference.
- Attach the Loadout screenshot and approved Urkaaaz UI assets as secondary
  references for the dark iron/wood panels, warm gold trim, typography,
  materials, and visual quality.
- Reuse the visual identity of existing Urkaaaz units and catapults when
  available. Do not redesign their canonical appearance.
- This is only a first-pass visual proposal for `green_frontier`. Do not
  generalize the mockups to other biomes or add alternate biome treatments.

## Output

- Generate exactly two separate static landscape mockups:
  - `match_result_victory.png`
  - `match_result_defeat.png`
- Canvas: exactly 1920 x 1080 pixels, 16:9.
- Save the files for review under `assets_src/match/result-mockups/`.
- Use the same popup size, placement, panel structure, typography, and content
  alignment in both variants. Change only the outcome treatment, humorous
  illustration, and outcome-specific available actions.
- These are composition references only. Do not import either mockup as a
  flattened runtime screen or Android drawable.

## Shared composition

- Keep the actual Green Frontier battlefield visible behind the popup,
  darkened enough to separate the foreground UI while preserving its green
  grass, natural colors, and rolling outdoor landscape. Do not replace it
  with a generic fantasy background.
- Center one compact, readable result panel over the battlefield. Use the
  established Urkaaaz dark translucent iron/wood surfaces, restrained rivets,
  warm-gold edging, and clear pale text.
- Keep the panel focused on the result. Do not add a full-screen dashboard,
  extra card grid, store, tutorial, or unrelated match statistics.
- Include a live-text header area for the outcome and a smaller context line
  for `LEVEL X · BIOME NAME`. Keep all production text, numbers, icons, and
  controls conceptually separate from the background and illustration.
- Add one friendly, humorous fantasy-game illustration that is clearly
  related to Green Frontier. It may overlap the panel edge, but must not
  obscure the result, reward information, or buttons.
- Use readable contrast and comfortable spacing. The popup must remain
  legible over Green Frontier's bright grass and foliage.

## Victory variant

- Show the exact outcome label `VICTORY!` prominently, with a warm gold
  highlight distinct from the defeat treatment.
- Use a playful, celebratory scene with recognizable existing Urkaaaz
  characters or a catapult. The joke should come from harmless slapstick or
  over-the-top celebration, not from gore, injury, or a new gameplay ability.
- Tie the gag to Green Frontier's grass, leaves, or rolling green landscape
  without changing character identities or inventing canonical lore.
- Include a clearly separated progression reward area for an item newly
  available in the next campaign level:
  - Label it `UNLOCKED IN LEVEL X` or `NEW IN LEVEL X`.
  - Show one dynamic unit or ammunition icon and its dynamic name.
  - Treat the item as data supplied by campaign progression. Do not invent,
    rename, or substitute an unlock.
  - Show this area only when the next-level progression actually supplies an
    unlock; otherwise leave the area out rather than showing a fake reward.
- Include a future gold-reward row labeled `GOLD EARNED`, but show an empty or
  neutral placeholder (`—`) instead of a number. Gold rewards are not
  implemented: do not imply that gold was granted, display a fabricated
  amount, or make the placeholder look interactive. The runtime UI should hide
  this row until gold rewards exist.
- Show two native-control placeholders:
  - Primary: `NEXT LEVEL`, available only when a next level exists and its
    completion has been persisted.
  - Secondary: `EXIT TO CAMPAIGN`.
- For the final campaign level, do not show `NEXT LEVEL`; use the campaign
  return action instead. Do not imply that a level beyond the campaign exists.

## Defeat variant

- Show the exact outcome label `DEFEAT` prominently, using a restrained
  ember-red or dark copper accent while retaining the shared panel style.
- Use a different friendly Green Frontier comic scene. Prefer an existing
  character in a harmless, mildly absurd setback (for example, helmet askew,
  tangled banner, or a harmless pile of leaves). Keep the tone encouraging
  rather than mocking; no gore, graphic injury, or humiliating imagery.
- Do not show a next-level unlock or claim campaign progression was granted.
- Keep the same reserved `GOLD EARNED` row with a neutral `—` placeholder
  only as a design reference; the runtime UI must hide it until rewards are
  implemented.
- Show two native-control placeholders:
  - Primary: `RETRY`.
  - Secondary: `EXIT TO CAMPAIGN`.
- Make the retry action visually primary without making the campaign exit
  difficult to find.

## Green Frontier art direction

- Derive colors, ambient details, and the illustration's visual gag from the
  supplied Green Frontier reference. Preserve the same biome in both
  outcomes.
- Use recognizable Green Frontier details such as grass, leaves, and the
  rolling outdoor landscape. Do not introduce snow, copperwood, ashland, or
  marsh scenery.
- Keep biome details inside the illustration or visible battlefield; do not
  tint the outcome text or reward icons so heavily that their meaning changes.
- Do not place the same joke or the same pose in both outcome variants.

## Runtime separation requirements

- Victory/defeat labels, level and biome names, gold values, unlock names,
  icons, and button labels are dynamic UI, not text baked into a production
  background.
- The UI must receive the match outcome and campaign progression result; it
  must not infer success from artwork or mutate campaign state itself.
- `NEXT LEVEL` is available only after successful campaign completion
  persistence and only when another level exists.
- `RETRY` restarts the current level. `EXIT TO CAMPAIGN` returns to campaign
  level selection; it must not be presented as quitting the app.
- The result popup must not claim a gold reward or unlock that the game did
  not report.

## Negative requirements

- No fake gold amount, fabricated unit/ammunition unlock, score, XP, kill
  count, campaign completion percentage, or unimplemented reward.
- No reward-shop or purchase action, premium currency, tutorial, settings,
  pause controls, or exit-to-operating-system action.
- No fictional Urkaaaz unit, ammunition, spell, faction, biome, logo, or lore.
- No gore, disturbing imagery, hostile real-world symbols, watermark,
  signature, phone frame, or presentation board.
- No alternate screen layouts: both outcomes must be directly comparable.
- No text or controls baked into any asset intended for runtime reuse.

## Review checklist

- Confirm there are exactly two 1920 x 1080 landscape PNG mockups.
- Confirm both use the same panel geometry and content hierarchy.
- Confirm the outcome is immediately distinguishable in both variants.
- Confirm both comic illustrations reflect Green Frontier and use familiar
  Urkaaaz characters or equipment.
- Confirm victory shows only a progression-provided next-level unlock and
  defeat shows none.
- Confirm the gold row is clearly a non-functional placeholder with no
  fabricated amount.
- Confirm the button labels and enabled states match the outcome and campaign
  boundary.
- Confirm dynamic text, values, reward icons, and controls remain separable
  for native Android implementation.
- Show both mockups individually for review before producing or integrating
  production assets.
